          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The consensus primarily associates kaph with divine care, protection, secure holding, and remembrance, while also recognizing contextual uses involving prayer, generosity, agency, and power.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book identifies kaph specifically as the open palm and unifies its symbolism around covering, carrying, offering, generosity, and prayer, contrasting it with yad as the hand of power.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"The palm as an image of protective care, holding, and remembrance, especially God's care for his people.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The open hand as the organ of covering, carrying, and offering rather than the hand of power.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_BROADENS\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book expands the dominant care-and-remembrance identification into a general open-hand category that includes charity, offering, and prayer. At the same time, it excludes or reassigns power and force to yad, whereas the consensus allows power and agency among kaph's contextual associations.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no direct contradiction over protective care: Exodus 33 and Isaiah 49 fit both readings. The material difference is the book's sharper lexical-symbolic division between open-handed kaph and power-oriented yad.\",\"rationale\":\"The book retains the consensus's central protective and caring imagery but treats it as one expression of a broader open-palm function. Prayer and generosity were already recognized consensus alternatives, so the reading is not novel. Its consolidation of those uses and rejection of power as properly belonging to kaph materially changes the category's boundaries, making the relationship refined rather than a match.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The cited passages illustrate covering, remembrance, and generosity, but they do not by themselves establish that openness is intrinsic to every use of kaph or that kaph and yad consistently divide into offering versus power. The printed references also do not directly support the entry's prayer claim.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
